The async `retry_all.__rand__` has a real bug. When `other` is a `retry_all`, it returns `None` instead of a flattened `retry_all`. The sync version correctly returns `retry_all(*other.retries, *self.retries)`, so the async one looks like a copy mistake.

The effect is that `retry_all(...) & async_retry_all` makes `__and__` call `other.__rand__(self)`, which returns `None`. The user then gets `None` as their retry strategy, which breaks at runtime. Pyright flags the same line (F1). The tests only cover the sync path, so they don't catch it.


